Invalidate assignment cache on modify operations

Backport of upstream commit b5abcae8e9 (master, 2026-07-15, Change-Id
I94b1b9a725575735cceac0c470168fa7d2a1f4e9). Keystone 30.0.0 contains
it, 29.0.0 does not, and no 29.0.x tag exists that could be pinned
instead.

Keystone 29.0.0 memoizes list_role_assignments in
COMPUTED_ASSIGNMENTS_REGION (keystone/assignment/core.py), but
create_system_grant_for_user and create_system_grant_for_group do not
invalidate it, and neither does the domain-deletion callback after
delete_domain_assignments. The keystone operator renders
[cache] enabled = true, so a listing cached before the grant answers
until [cache] expiration_time passes (oslo.cache default 600 s). K-ORC
lists a RoleAssignment before it creates it and reads it back after the
create, so a system-scoped RoleAssignment stays unavailable for up to
ten minutes. That holds AccountReady on the KeystoneService that asks
for a system role, and with it GlanceReady on a ControlPlane that
enforces Glance quotas (#732): the glance service user needs reader on
the system to read every project's limits.

The patch adds COMPUTED_ASSIGNMENTS_REGION.invalidate() after the
driver call in both create_system_grant_for_* methods and after
delete_domain_assignments, as upstream does.

Upstream added no test, and no upstream unit test pins the missing
invalidation. The patch therefore carries no test hunk.

tests/container-images/verify_keystone.sh (test_system_grant_patch_applied)
asserts against the built image that each system grant invalidates the
region once.

No 2026.2 twin: 30.0.0 carries upstream change 997392.

Applies to keystone 29.0.0 (2026.1).

Upstream status: merged on master as
https://review.opendev.org/c/openstack/keystone/+/997392; not
backported to stable/2026.1.

